==Icelandic==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|non|is}} {{term|níu|lang=non}} (whence also Danish {{term|ni|lang=da}}; Faroese {{term|níggju|lang=fo}} and Swedish {{term|nio|lang=sv}}) from {{proto|Germanic|niwun|lang=is}} from {{proto|Indo-European|h₁néwn̥|lang=is}}. Cognates with Gothic {{term|𐌽𐌹𐌿𐌽}}; Old English {{term|nigon|lang=ang}} (English {{term|nine|lang=en}}); Old Frisian {{term|nigun}} (West Frisian {{term|njoggen}}); Old High German {{term|niun}} (German {{term|neun|lang=de}}).

===Numeral===
'''níu''' {{indecl}}

# '''[[nine]]'''; the cardinal number after [[átta#Icelandic|átta]] and before [[tíu#Icelandic|tíu]].

